Z||000015||sqoach||08-29-2001||12:59 AM||||Ok, it sounds like a really fast tapping-on-metal. And it seems to only happen when I accelerate slow. (at least that's the only time I notice it) I am almost positive it's within the engine, and not a rattle from something being loose.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||08-29-2001||01:15 AM||||I'm thinking trans-axle... Either that, or your balancer might be dead... If it's internal, and that loud, something's seriously wrong. If it's only happening on the bottome end of the RPM band, and only at part-throttle, I'd say that you've got a mixture problem, and your head's gunked up, causing ignition pinging. It changes tone from whatever car you drive:<BR>Our van makes a musical "ti-oink" sound, our Dodge raider (before it tossed a rod. Long story) make a solid ball-peen-hammer-on-chunk-of-lead sound, and my mazda made a noise that sounded like a bad lifter... Go figure...<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000017||Joe Torma||08-29-2001||01:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>Ok, it sounds like a really fast tapping-on-metal. And it seems to only happen when I accelerate slow. (at least that's the only time I notice it) I am almost positive it's within the engine, and not a rattle from something being loose.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Use 93 octane gas+Octane booster and see if you still get it.<P>Sounds(not like I've listen to it) like pinging to me.  Are you saying it happens when the engine is under load?  Clean your injectors like mentioned, check your timing and EGR.<P>Joe<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Tonker||08-29-2001||02:57 AM||||I get something similar on acceleration, but it sounds to me like an electrical noise. Something like a faint crackling coming from behind me. I think it has to do with dampness in the engine compartment since I notice it after washing the car or after it's been rained on. Seems to go away after a few minutes though.||||reg||5
Z||000019||sqoach||08-29-2001||02:26 PM||||What do you mean by "under load?" I've put in a couple bottles of that fuel injector cleaner since I've had the car, about two months. Just put in a bottle a few days ago. Does that stuff really help? Anyway, hasn't seemed to help my problem. Also filled up with 91 octane last two times. That's getting expensive  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> Haven't used any octane boost. Probably because I've never heard of it. I had someone check the timing for me, and they said it was ok. I haven't changed the tranny fluid since I got the car. Should I do that? What do I use??<P>Tonker, I guess that's another way to describe it. Kind of a crackling noise. But it happens all the time.||||reg||1
Z||000020||sqoach||08-30-2001||10:59 AM||||*bump*||||reg||1
Z||000021||87GTZ34||08-30-2001||11:17 AM||||Sqoach, Be careful with using injector cleaner too often and too strong (not properly diluted). Marren motorsports told me that those cleaners eat away at the coating on the wire that makes up the solenoid in the injector, eventually shorting and destroying the injector.<P>Can you reproduce the rattle with car stopped, brakes on, and ride the clutch or if auto give it some gas?  If you can then you will be able to hear the rattle from the engine compartment while your 'buddy' makes it rattle from the driver's seat.<P>If it is a ping then you have one or more of the following: timings off, wrong pistons, wrong plugs, mucho carbon, or fuel problems (too lean).  If it is a ping then start out with checking the timing and don't ASSume that the harmonic balancer timing mark is correct.  Check that 1st with the old clean plastic drinking straw trick in the #1 cyl thru the plug hole.  Check TDC mark on balancer and have a 'buddy' watch the straw for the highest position.  Do this on a cold engine obviously.  Next is a compression test and check the plugs for proper heat range and spark gap while they're out.  Also look at/read the plugs for color.  If there is a whole lot'o pinging going on, you will be able to tell from reading the plugs and it may be isolated to only one or a couple of cylinders.  After that you will need to hook up an anlayzer and check the O2, and all of the block learn settings to check the fuel. GL<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Mach10||08-30-2001||01:00 PM||||"Under load:" As in it'll only do this when the engine is "working" and not when it's freewheeling. As in when it's accelerating: I.e., working to move the car forward.||||reg||7
Z||000023||sqoach||08-30-2001||01:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Sqoach, Be careful with using injector cleaner too often and too strong (not properly diluted). Marren motorsports told me that those cleaners eat away at the coating on the wire that makes up the solenoid in the injector, eventually shorting and destroying the injector.<P>Can you reproduce the rattle with car stopped, brakes on, and ride the clutch or if auto give it some gas?  If you can then you will be able to hear the rattle from the engine compartment while your 'buddy' makes it rattle from the driver's seat.<P>If it is a ping then you have one or more of the following: timings off, wrong pistons, wrong plugs, mucho carbon, or fuel problems (too lean).  If it is a ping then start out with checking the timing and don't ASSume that the harmonic balancer timing mark is correct.  Check that 1st with the old clean plastic drinking straw trick in the #1 cyl thru the plug hole.  Check TDC mark on balancer and have a 'buddy' watch the straw for the highest position.  Do this on a cold engine obviously.  Next is a compression test and check the plugs for proper heat range and spark gap while they're out.  Also look at/read the plugs for color.  If there is a whole lot'o pinging going on, you will be able to tell from reading the plugs and it may be isolated to only one or a couple of cylinders.  After that you will need to hook up an anlayzer and check the O2, and all of the block learn settings to check the fuel. GL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, I've heard using too much could hurt rather than help. I make sure I fill it up when I put the stuff in, though. Thanks for the advice. I'll have to get someone to help me out. I'm not sure I've heard of the plastic straw trick. ??<P>Mach10, thanks for the explanation. Yes it only happens when in gear. When it's in neutral, I don't hear it.<P>Also, should I change my tranny fluid? If so what kind? 82K miles on the car.||||reg||1
