Z||000025||Mach10||08-30-2001||02:22 PM||||Shhhh! Don't upset the poor lad!||||reg||12
Z||000026||87GTZ34||08-30-2001||03:38 PM||||Sqoach, Have your oldest sis help you out and make sure you take lots of picks (and post em)!  Seriously though, I would definitely try to hear it from the eng compartment before you touch it with a wrench.  Either use a couple of long socket extensions and put one end on the motor (where you think the noise is coming from but not dist, coil etc.) and the other to the little triangle lobe in front of the ear canal.  You also can use a section of garden hose but have better success with the extensions.  This will xmit internal engine noises so you can hear them more clearly.<BR>as always GL<P>P.S. Note to Cliff - the spellchecker won't fire up.||||reg||1
Z||000027||sqoach||08-30-2001||03:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierodave:<BR><B>How many miles on the engine?  sounds to me like you may have worn Rod or main bearings.   Do you have over a 100k on the engine?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oops. Guess I should have said car/engine, instead of just car. 82K miles on the car and engine. No engine swap that I know of.||||reg||1
Z||000028||sqoach||08-30-2001||03:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Sqoach, Have your oldest sis help you out and make sure you take lots of picks (and post em)!  Seriously though, I would definitely try to hear it from the eng compartment before you touch it with a wrench.  Either use a couple of long socket extensions and put one end on the motor (where you think the noise is coming from but not dist, coil etc.) and the other to the little triangle lobe in front of the ear canal.  You also can use a section of garden hose but have better success with the extensions.  This will xmit internal engine noises so you can hear them more clearly.<BR>as always GL<P>P.S. Note to Cliff - the spellchecker won't fire up.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL! Sorry 87GT, she is not a car person. And she doesn't live with us.<BR>Anyway, I'll try that and see what happens. I don't know much history on this car, so I'm not sure what's been done and what's not. I know that the plugs and wires have been replaced. I changed the oil not too long after I got it. It runs just fine. Not lacking power. Of course taking the converter out helped  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000029||JetroGT||08-30-2001||04:04 PM||||I get a rattle at low accl too. But it sounds like it's comming from the pasengers side dash board. almost sounds like a marble rolling around in a can. 86 SE<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||87GTZ34||08-31-2001||04:53 PM||||Okay I checked out my rattle situation today.  Sqoach along with timing advance upon engine load, the motor also advances... I had three rattles and fixed 2 of them.  The 1st was the muffler/trunk shield was just touching the muffler so when the exhaust system would vibrate I got a little rattle.  The second was the exhaust springs on the front right.  The Borla comes w/o the hangers welded on and when they put it on several months ago they put it so the springs weren't stretched much and one of them rattled.  So when the eng (including exhaust) would move slightly upon acceleration, those two rattles would start to rattle.  The 3rd is what I mentioned previously that the AC junction block is not fastened properly to the frame and rattles more when there is pressure in the AC lines.  The actual rattle is brake line that runs across the AC block and the brake line 'rub spring' is rattling on the frame.  I'll fix that one once I figure out what is used to fasten it.  So the moral of this long story is... put your car up on ramps (rearend) and look for shields, exhaust, brake lines, water pipes etc. that are close when parked but may touch when the engine mounts flex.  Also, make sure your mounts aren't flexing too much (mine are all new).  How's that for for a Fiero WE project? GL||||reg||1
Z||000031||sqoach||09-02-2001||05:25 PM||||Ok, I put the rear up on jackstands, blocked the front wheels, and started it up. Put it in gear to see if I could hear that sound at 1500-2000 RPM....nothing. Even tried it in 2nd gear, still didn't hear it. I checked a few shields/hangers. They seem to be ok. I'll check again when the engines cools down. Also just noticed a squeak from my exhaust at idle. Coming from driver's side. Not sure if the two are related.||||reg||1
